---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';
import { getPracticeURL, withPracticeURL } from '../../lib/practice';
import axeViolation from '../../samples/axe-violation.txt?raw';
import ruleButtonName from '../../samples/wcag-rule-button-name.txt?raw';
import ruleHeadingOrder from '../../samples/wcag-rule-heading-order.txt?raw';

const practiceURL = getPracticeURL(Astro);
const toc = [
   { id: 'read', label: 'Read the violation' },
   { id: 'element', label: 'Find the element' },
   { id: 'fix', label: 'Find the fix' },
   { id: 'impact', label: 'Decide what to fix first' },
   { id: 'verify', label: 'Confirm the fix' },
   { id: 'unmapped', label: 'Rules with no criterion' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Fix what a scan found"
   summary="<p>A violation names a rule, how serious it is, and the elements that failed. This guide goes from that report to a fix you have confirmed with a screen reader.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="read">Read the violation</h2>
      <p>
         <code>a1 axe</code> runs <a href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core">axe-core</a>
         inside the page in a real browser, so it tests what the browser rendered and not
         only the HTML the server sent. The examples here scan the docs'
         <a href="/guides/demo">practice page</a>, a demo page with known problems.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 axe {practiceURL}</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{withPracticeURL(axeViolation, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>button-name</code></dt>
         <dd>The axe rule that failed.</dd>
         <dt><code>critical</code></dt>
         <dd>The impact: how badly this kind of failure affects a user.</dd>
         <dt><code>WCAG 4.1.2 (A)</code></dt>
         <dd>The success criterion the rule tests, and its level. A success criterion is one testable requirement of the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines.</dd>
         <dt>Failing elements</dt>
         <dd>A CSS selector for each element, its HTML, and the ways to satisfy the rule.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         <a href="/reference/cli#check"><code>a1 check</code></a> runs the same scan and adds
         the criteria that need a person. Use <code>a1 axe</code> when you want only the
         scan, several pages at once, or a report file.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="element">Find the element</h2>
      <p>
         To see the element on the page, paste the selector into the browser console.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="Browser console"><code>{`document.querySelector('tr[data-status="pending"] > td:nth-child(5) > .icon-button')`}</code></pre>
      <p>
         "Fix any of the following" lists every way to satisfy the rule. You need one of
         them, and axe cannot know which suits your design.
      </p>
      <p>
         With <code>--json</code>, each violation has <code>id</code>, <code>impact</code>,
         <code>help</code>, <code>helpUrl</code>, <code>tags</code>, and a <code>nodes</code>
         array. Each node has <code>target</code>, <code>html</code>, and
         <code>failureSummary</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="fix">Find the fix</h2>
      <p><code>a1 wcag rule</code> maps an axe rule to the criterion it tests.</p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag rule button-name</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{ruleButtonName.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Then <code>a1 wcag 4.1.2</code> prints the criterion: what it requires, how to test
         it, and the W3C techniques that satisfy it. For an icon button the technique is
         ARIA14, an <code>aria-label</code>.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="HTML"><code>{`<button type="button" class="icon-button" aria-label="Remove Dana Whitfield">\n   <svg aria-hidden="true">...</svg>\n</button>`}</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>a1 check</code> prints the <code>a1 wcag rule</code> command for every failing
         rule, on the <strong>Fix it</strong> line.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="impact">Decide what to fix first</h2>
      <p>
         Impact comes from axe. Each rule has one fixed impact. It describes how bad that
         kind of failure is, not how many people meet it on your page.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>critical</code></dt>
         <dd>Blocks access. A button or image with no name cannot be used: <code>button-name</code>, <code>image-alt</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>serious</code></dt>
         <dd>The content is there and hard to get to: <code>color-contrast</code>, <code>link-name</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>moderate</code></dt>
         <dd>Confusing without blocking: <code>heading-order</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>minor</code></dt>
         <dd>Noise: <code>empty-heading</code>.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         <code>--fail-on serious</code> makes <code>a1 axe</code> and <code>a1 check</code>
         exit 4 only for serious and critical violations. Use it to keep a build passing
         while you work through the rest.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="verify">Confirm the fix</h2>
      <p>Run the scan again for that rule, then check what a screen reader announces.</p>
      <pre><code>a1 axe {practiceURL}fixed/ --rule button-name
a1 sr start --sr virtual {practiceURL}fixed/
a1 sr elements button
a1 sr stop</code></pre>
      <p>
         The scan tells you the button has a name. The screen reader tells you what the name
         is. "button, Remove Dana Whitfield" is a fix. "button, button" or "button, icon"
         passes the scan and still does not tell the user what the button does.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="unmapped">Rules with no criterion</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag rule heading-order</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{ruleHeadingOrder.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         axe tags some rules as best practice. No WCAG criterion requires them, and they are
         still worth fixing. A default scan leaves them out. Run one by name with
         <code>--rule</code>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 axe {practiceURL} --rule heading-order</code></pre>
   </section>
</DocShell>
